      "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1457979?v=4",
      "gravatar_id": "",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/bzbarsky",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/following{/other_user}",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/gists{/gist_id}",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/starred{/owner}{/repo}",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/events{/privacy}",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/bzbarsky/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "user_view_type": "public",
      "site_admin": false
    },
    "created_at": "2017-08-12T00:15:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-08-12T00:15:07Z",
    "body": "If we're just talking about implementation in Gecko, there are ways to make this work.  For example, we could have the scrollable area inside the input inherit the vertical padding and force the used vertical padding on the input itself to 0, but do nothing to the horizontal padding.\r\n\r\nThe question is what the spec is supposed to say here, given that it doesn't really have a concept of a scrollable thing separate from the input itself, but that's somewhat orthogonal to what we do in Gecko, where we _do_ have such a distinction.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
